WHICH PAIR THIS LOG CERTIFIES. This log is COMMITTED on the branch (R7-L3),
so it cannot certify the commit that carries it: the train's gate A needs
the reviewed code SHA to BE the branch tip. Every part below ran with the
branch at the CODE commit (the run record's HEAD, checked by this writer),
and it certifies the pair it names -- the sql commit and the code commit.
Three commits follow on the branch: the .gitignore negations, the three
round-8 logs and the route manifest's re-pin, none of them a file the train
reads or one the api imports. The pair then moves to the final tip, and
the same plan, check and controls are re-run there to
autolog-hotfix-r8-trainplan-final.log beside the notes, untracked for the
reason above (notes 19, D-19.6).

The plan is READ-ONLY. `--go` is never passed, here or anywhere in this
round's evidence. The plan wrote no release state: section 3's CONTROL E
asserts that as a control rather than in prose, against a listing of the
state directory and the state file's sha256 on each side of the run.

THE ROUTE DISCRIMINATOR. The route is POST-only and every probe in phase 7
is a GET, so the reading that tells the two builds apart is

    GET /api/v1/logs/auto   ->  404 on the build WITHOUT the route
                            ->  405 on the build WITH it
                            ->  never 200, on either build

The dry plan below stops at the 404 because the route is not deployed. The
405 half is READ IN PROCESS in section 5: this tree's own application
object, its middleware included, through the train's own `code_state` --
405 with the route, 404 with it removed in memory. On a box it is proven
only by a deploy, and that reading is recorded as one (notes 19).

THE DERIVED SCHEMA (M2), THE CURRENT POSITION (M1 / B9) AND 350'S POLICY
(R7-M1, R7-M2, R7-M3). Every object probe asks about the schema
`assert_roles_resolve_one_schema` derives for the migration role and every
api role. The sequence probe reads where the sequence IS, through the
accounted-value query and against the floor it reads OUT OF 350 at the
reviewed precursor -- one policy, one copy -- after checking that the
catalogue carries exactly the columns 350 declares; and it asks for cache 1
and a logged relation as 350 does. The dry plan reaches the derivation once
-- phase 3, on the primary, before its `--go` return -- and reaches no
object probe that reads the position: that runs in phase 3's postcondition
and phase 7's closing re-read, which a dry plan does not reach. So section 4
executes the train copy's own functions against local servers, each refusal
beside a mutant that removes its check, section 6 runs the read-only
`check`, which derives the schema and runs every object probe on BOTH
production boxes, and section 7 reads the pool-retention controls (R7-M4).

Local paths are post-processed to stable tokens -- this worktree reads WT,
the main checkout REPO, this lane's scratch TMP -- so every command stays
runnable while carrying no workstation layout (B11). The controller's
machine name and the two per-box inventory selectors are replaced by
<CONTROLLER>, <PRIMARY-SELECTOR> and <STANDBY-SELECTOR>; the tally closes
this log, and the writer refuses if any spelling of them survives.

